      "claim": "Rosaramicin, a complex polycyclic macrolide with multiple ester/lactone functionalities, has an absolute oral bioavailability of 39% (range 27–53%) after a 500-mg tablet dose in humans, supporting that structurally complex polycyclic lactones can achieve F ≥ 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C47"],
      "claim": "Idarubicin, a large polycyclic anthracycline with multiple hydroxyls and ketones, has an oral bioavailability of 39% in humans, indicating that complex, moderately lipophilic polycyclic natural products can be orally bioavailable above the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C18"],
      "claim": "Artesunate, a semi-synthetic artemisinin derivative, shows an absolute oral bioavailability of 61% in patients, demonstrating that even endoperoxide-containing polycyclic lactones can have high oral bioavailability.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C20"],
      "claim": "5-Hydroxyrofecoxib, a smaller lactone-containing molecule, has an oral bioavailability of only 14%, but its structural dissimilarity (aromatic sulfone, no polycyclic ether/lactone network) limits transferability to the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a complex polycyclic lactone/ether natural-product-like molecule. Analogous macrolides (rosaramicin ~39%), anthracyclines (idarubicin ~30–39%), and artemisinin derivatives (artesunate 61%) support F ≥ 20%. The few low-bioavailability examples are structurally distant or salt/formulation-specific. Overall, high oral bioavailability is better supported.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental bioavailability data exist for the exact query scaffold.",
